faq:
  - q: "What should I do immediately after being laid off?"
    a: "Five things in the first 48 hours: (1) Negotiate your severance — most severance offers are negotiable, especially at senior levels. Do not sign immediately. (2) Understand your benefits continuation — COBRA timeline, equity vesting, and any accelerated vesting provisions. (3) Update your LinkedIn to open-to-work (recruiter-only mode) immediately — inbound starts within days. (4) Email your network before you need anything — let former managers and colleagues know you are exploring and what you are looking for. (5) Do not make major financial decisions in the first two weeks. The emotional volatility of a layoff distorts risk calculus."
  - q: "How do you explain a layoff in an interview?"
    a: "Directly and briefly — then pivot to your narrative. The best answer: 'The company went through a [restructuring / RIF / round of layoffs] that affected my team. It was not performance-related — I was [leading X / delivering Y] at the time. What I'm focused on now is [specific next step].' Never over-explain, be defensive, or speak negatively about the company. Layoffs are common and understood. What interviewers are listening for is how you carry yourself, not the fact of the layoff itself."
  - q: "How long does a job search take after a layoff?"
    a: "With active, systematic outreach — 6–10 weeks to first offer is realistic for mid-to-senior professionals. Without a clear strategy (applying to everything, waiting for inbound, no network activation), 3–6 months is more typical. The difference is almost entirely in the quality of positioning and the volume of proactive outreach in the first 30 days. The first 30 days set the pace for the entire search."
  - q: "Should I take a gap or start searching immediately?"
    a: "Start the search immediately, even if you plan to take a gap. Reasons: (1) You can always decline opportunities — you cannot undo weeks of delay. (2) Market timing is unpredictable. Starting fast gives you optionality. (3) Momentum is hard to rebuild once it breaks. If you want a true gap, take it after landing an offer and negotiating a deferred start date — not before you have started your search."
  - q: "How do I negotiate severance after a layoff?"
    a: "You have more leverage than you think — and almost nothing to lose by asking. Common negotiable items: additional weeks of base pay, equity acceleration (accelerated vesting of unvested shares), extended COBRA coverage, outplacement services, a title change for better positioning (if you were laid off as an IC, can you leave as a [Senior/Lead]?), and reference language. Most companies have a standard severance formula and a band of flexibility. Ask specifically: 'Is there flexibility on the number of weeks given my tenure and level?'"
